    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">First unprovoked seizure (R56.9)</content>. The prolonged confusion afterward, the minute of rhythmic jerking and the vomiting point to an epileptic seizure rather than convulsive syncope, in which jerks are brief and recovery is quick; the ECG was normal. By the ILAE 1964 classification it is an unknown-onset motor (tonic-clonic) seizure, because nobody saw how it began. No acute symptomatic cause was found: sodium, glucose and calcium were normal and he was not intoxicated or withdrawing from a drug, so sleep loss, heat, missed meals and cannabis are counted as triggers, not causes. MRI and EEG are normal, there was no earlier brain injury and the seizure happened while he was awake, so his risk of another seizure is at the lower end of the 21% to 45% over two years. He does not have epilepsy. Dr. Abernathy-Leung explained that starting a medicine now would lower the two-year risk but would not change the long-term outlook, and that side effects affect 7% to 31% of people. He chose not to start one, and both clinicians agree. [S]<list><item>No antiseizure medicine now; a second unprovoked seizure would mean epilepsy and a medicine such as levetiracetam or lamotrigine.</item><item>No driving; Hong Kong&apos;s licence rules apply, and the clinic advises at least six months without a seizure.</item><item>Seizure first aid taught to his colleagues; no work at heights or swimming alone.</item><item>Sleep, meals, heat and fluid plan; no cannabis while the risk is highest, which he chose.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure recurrence, auras, staring spells and jerks on waking (event diary)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No events since the seizure; event diary started</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Neurological examination</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal; no focal signs</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>MRI of the brain with an epilepsy protocol</td><td>After a first seizure; repeat only with a new event or finding</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal with an epilepsy protocol</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>EEG (routine, and sleep-deprived if the first is normal or delayed)</td><td>Soon after a first seizure; repeat only with a new event</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Sleep-deprived EEG normal; no epileptiform discharges</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sodium, glucose, calcium and magnesium</td><td>At the event; repeat if abnormal or with a new event</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Emergency department sodium 136 mmol/L and glucose 104 mg/dL; clinic sodium 139, calcium 9.5 and magnesium 2.0 four days later</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Creatine kinase and creatinine after a convulsive seizure with exertion</td><td>Until normal (about one week, then as needed)</td><td>Due</td><td>Creatine kinase 820 U/L and creatinine 1.02 mg/dL four days after the seizure, falling; repeat in about a week</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>ECG with QTc (syncope versus seizure)</td><td>At the event; repeat if symptoms suggest a cardiac cause</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Sinus bradycardia 56, QTc 412 ms at the first visit; no cardiac cause suspected</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicine decision</td><td>After the evaluation, and again with any new seizure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Shared decision: no medicine after a first unprovoked seizure with a normal MRI and EEG</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicines that lower the seizure threshold or cause withdrawal seizures</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No tramadol, bupropion or sedative muscle relaxants; he takes only acetaminophen</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Triggers: sleep, meals, heat, fluids and electrolytes, alcohol and cannabis</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Plan for at least seven hours of sleep, regular meals, breaks and air conditioning in the booth, and drinking to thirst with salt in the heat; no cannabis</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Driving and dangerous activities</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not driving; work at heights and swimming alone paused</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure first aid and safety at work</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>First aid handout for the studio; call for an ambulance if a seizure lasts over five minutes, repeats or causes injury</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Epilepsy specialist consultation</td><td>After a first seizure</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Abernathy-Leung today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>No further seizures. In progress; target 1973-07-13 [S]</item><item>Sleep at least seven hours a night, including on film schedules. In progress; target 1973-06-15 [S]</item><item>No cannabis while the seizure risk is highest. In progress; target 1973-07-13 [S]</item><item>Back pain at 3 or less out of 10 with full training. In progress; target 1973-07-13 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults: Report of the Guideline Development Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology and the American Epilepsy Society (2015); Practice Parameter: evaluating an apparent unprovoked first seizure in adults (an evidence-based review) (2007); Operational classification of seizure types by the International League Against Epilepsy: Position Paper of the ILAE Commission for Classification and Terminology (2017); ILAE official report: a practical clinical definition of epilepsy (2014); Recommendation for a definition of acute symptomatic seizure (2010); Supplementary Health Information Sheet for Driver Assessment (First Schedule of the Road Traffic (Driving Licences) Regulations, Cap. 374B) (2021); Neurological disorders: assessing fitness to drive (first unprovoked epileptic seizure) (2026); First Aid for Seizures (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Creatine kinase and creatinine at the laboratory in about a week. Review in four weeks. Call at once for any further event; ambulance for a seizure over five minutes or a second seizure. Next scheduled visit 1973-06-15: one-month review: no further events, sleep and heat plan, kidney function and creatine kinase, back pain program. [S]</paragraph></text>

